    "# 🟢 IBM Quantum Safe Beginner Lab: Cryptographic Discovery & CBOM Generation\n",
    "\n",
    "## 🎯 Learning Objectives\n",
    "1. Understand the concept of the **Discover** phase in the IBM Quantum Safe Transformation Framework.\n",
    "2. Build a codebase scanner that automatically parses and identifies cryptographic algorithms in source files.\n",
    "3. Classify algorithm risk levels against post-quantum standards (RSA vs. ML-KEM, ECDSA vs. ML-DSA).\n",
    "4. Export cryptographic metadata to a standardized, OWASP CycloneDX-compliant **Cryptographic Bill of Materials (CBOM)** JSON format."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "## 🧱 Task 1: Complete the Cryptographic Algorithm Risk Classifier\n",
    "Your first task is to write a classifier function `classify_crypto_algorithm(algorithm_name, key_size)`. This function is critical for evaluating whether an algorithm is quantum-vulnerable, categorizing its NIST post-quantum category, and recommending the appropriate upgrade path.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Rules for Classification:\n",
    "- **RSA**, **Diffie-Hellman (DH)**, and **3DES** are **always quantum-vulnerable**.\n",
    "- **ECDSA**, **ECC**, and **secp256k1** are **always quantum-vulnerable**.\n",
    "- **AES** is quantum-vulnerable **only if key_size < 256**.\n",
    "- **ML-KEM** and **Kyber** are quantum-safe and map to **FIPS 203**.\n",
    "- **ML-DSA** and **Dilithium** are quantum-safe and map to **FIPS 204**.\n",
    "- Set appropriate `recommended_action` targets: `'Migrate to ML-KEM'` (for encryption/key exchange), `'Migrate to ML-DSA'` (for signatures), `'Upgrade to AES-256'`, or `'None'`."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
